 "chunks": [
  {
   "text": "Why would primes care where zeros are?",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 2.95
  },
  {
   "text": "Because of an exact formula, the explicit formula,",
   "start": 2.95,
   "end": 5.9
  },
  {
   "text": "from Riemann's paper, proved in this form by von Mangoldt.",
   "start": 5.9,
   "end": 9.612
  },
  {
   "text": "Count prime powers, each weighted by the log of its prime:",
   "start": 9.612,
   "end": 13.062
  },
  {
   "text": "that's ψ(x), a cleaner staircase.",
   "start": 13.062,
   "end": 15.787
  },
  {
   "text": "The formula says psi of x is x, minus one wave for each pair of zeros,",
   "start": 15.787,
   "end": 20.275
  },
  {
   "text": "plus a tiny correction.",
   "start": 20.275,
   "end": 22.025
  },
  {
   "text": "A zero at one half plus i gamma, with its mirror partner,",
   "start": 22.975,
   "end": 26.525
  },
  {
   "text": "adds a wave that oscillates with frequency gamma in log x.",
   "start": 26.525,
   "end": 29.888
  },
  {
   "text": "Start with the smooth part.",
   "start": 29.888,
   "end": 31.638
  },
  {
   "text": "Add the first wave.",
   "start": 31.638,
   "end": 33.175
  },
  {
   "text": "Ten.",
   "start": 33.175,
   "end": 33.888
  },
  {
   "text": "A hundred.",
   "start": 33.888,
   "end": 34.788
  },
  {
   "text": "A thousand.",
   "start": 34.788,
   "end": 35.925
  },
  {
   "text": "The prime staircase comes out of pure waves.",
   "start": 35.925,
   "end": 38.838
  },
  {
   "text": "The zeros are the frequencies; the primes are the signal.",
   "start": 38.838,
   "end": 42.45
  }
 ],
